bservable orders. I'm not sure that line belongs there, and I want to see whether it could cause the upstream sort to be dropped incorrectly.\n\nI'm checking whether the empty output for the ternary mask would be safe when deordering is allowed. Since the consumer only cares about the multiset of values, a plain elementwise expression shouldn't depend on input order, and the cum_sum case is handled the same way in streaming because the optimizer logic is shared.\n\nI'm going to check how the sort gets dropped in mod.rs, specifically the simplify_ordering path.\n\n", "thinking_blocks": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "I'm looking at the ternary block, where output_observable is computed only from the truthy and falsy branches, before the mask is added. With a literal `when(x>1).then(1).otherwise(0)`, both branches are scalars, so output_observable comes out empty, which looks like the bug: the mask's ordering never gets included. That empty result would make the upstream sort look unobservable and get it removed, and I'm about to check what happens when the then-branch is col(\"x\") instead.\n\nChecking the deorder branch, I notice it forces output_observable to empty when the node is the ternary mask, which looks suspicious. I'm going to compare it against what I remember of the upstream polars code for the ternary and binary expression case.\n\nI'm checking whether setting output_observable to empty when the ternary mask is the single ordered node is right, since in a deorder context the returned value would be used as the projection's observable orders. I'm not sure that line belongs there, and I want to see whether it could cause the upstream sort to be dropped incorrectly.\n\nI'm checking whether the empty output for the ternary mask would be safe when deordering is allowed. Since the consumer only cares about the multiset of values, a plain elementwise expression shouldn't depend on input order, and the cum_sum case is handled the same way in streaming becaus